<Page path="/jpeg-webp-avif-png/">
  <h1>JPEG, WebP, AVIF or PNG: which format for a small file</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    No format is best at everything. Which one gives the smallest file at the same look depends on the picture and on the size
    you are aiming for, which is why KVGrainy tries all of them instead of guessing.
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Format</th><th>Good at</th><th>Watch out for</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>JPEG</strong></td>
        <td>Photographs. Opens in everything, ever. Hard to beat when the limit is generous.</td>
        <td>No transparency. Visible blocks and ringing at low quality.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>WebP</strong></td>
        <td>
          Photographs and graphics, with or without transparency, and animation. Smaller than JPEG at the same look, especially
          under tight limits.
        </td>
        <td>A few older programs and some upload forms still reject it.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>AVIF</strong></td>
        <td>Usually the smallest file for a photograph at a given look, with transparency too.</td>
        <td>
          Slow to produce, so KVGrainy only tries it on Best effort. Current browsers open it; much older software and some
          upload forms do not.
        </td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>PNG</strong></td>
        <td>Screenshots, logos and flat-colour graphics, where it is lossless and small. Keeps transparency exactly.</td>
        <td>Large for photographs, because it never throws detail away.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>What we saw</h2>
  <p>
    On ten real phone photos with a 100 KB limit, JPEG and WebP won on Quick effort (WebP for the smoother pictures, JPEG for the
    detailed ones). On Best effort, where AVIF also competes, it won on the 3-megapixel photos by about 4 to 5 points of the
    SSIMULACRA2 quality score, a difference you can see at that size. With a 2 MB limit, a lossless PNG won for all of the
    3-megapixel photos, because there was room to keep every pixel. Details are in the
    <a href="/compress-image-to-100kb/">100 KB</a>, <a href="/compress-image-to-1mb/">1 MB</a> and
    <a href="/compress-image-to-2mb/">2 MB</a> guides.
  </p>

  <h2>Rules of thumb</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A photo, tight limit, and it only has to open on modern devices: <strong>let KVGrainy choose</strong>; expect WebP or AVIF.</li>
    <li>A photo that must open anywhere (an old CMS, an email client, a printer shop): <strong>force JPEG</strong>.</li>
    <li>A screenshot, a logo, or anything with text and flat colour: <strong>PNG</strong> or WebP; avoid JPEG, which smears edges.</li>
    <li>Needs a transparent background: <strong>PNG, WebP or AVIF</strong>; JPEG would fill it with white.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why not just pick one?</h2>
  <p>
    Because the winner changes with the picture and with the limit: a flat screenshot beats a photo codec as PNG, a smooth photo
    wins with WebP at 100 KB, and a generous limit may favour lossless. KVGrainy runs the search for each format and keeps the
    best-looking result under your limit; see <a href="/how-it-works/">how it works</a>. You can always override it with the
    Format box.
  </p>
</Page>
